Package: caEditR
Title: Cell-Type-Resolved RNA-Editing Deconvolution (caRD-Edit, caNRD-Edit, TCA_Like)
Version: 0.99.0
Authors@R: person("Haim Krupkin+Claude", role = c("aut", "cre"), email = "haim.krupkin@gmail.com")
Description: Deconvolves bulk RNA-editing-ratio matrices into per-cell-type
    editing estimates using three interchangeable methods sharing the same
    underlying Bayesian per-site estimator: caRD_edit() (reference-based,
    needs a real sorted-cell-derived mu/sigma2/theta reference), caNRD_edit()
    (no-reference, self-estimates mu/sigma2 from the bulk cohort itself),
    and TCA_Like() (a thin wrapper around the real CRAN 'TCA' package, run
    with proportion-only mixing weights). Also provides
    estimate_proportions_signature_matrix(), a general
    NNLS-based (CIBERSORT-style, not literally CIBERSORT) alternative that
    works with any signature matrix (e.g. LM22, supplied by the user, or
    the bundled real GSE107011-derived alternative), for when a full
    multi-subject reference isn't available or appropriate,
    map_sites_to_genes()/build_coverage_from_expression() for
    mapping RNA-editing sites to their host gene (hg19/hg38, via the real
    Bioconductor UCSC TxDb annotation packages) and approximating a
    per-site coverage matrix from per-sample gene expression, and
    simulate_reference_and_cohort(), a fast synthetic-data generator for
    benchmarking all three methods against known ground truth. The core math (compute_effective_weights, binomial_tau2,
    deconvolve_site) is vendored, UNMODIFIED, from this project's own
    validated Python implementation (src/catca_edit/core.py,
    src/catca_edit/no_reference.py) and called in a fresh Python subprocess
    per operation (via base R's system2(), with JSON in/out via jsonlite) --
    this package does not re-implement that math in R, to avoid introducing
    new bugs into already-tested code, and deliberately does NOT embed
    Python inside the R process (e.g. via reticulate): doing so was found,
    during development, to fail unpredictably in long-running R sessions
    where R's own process had already loaded an incompatible system
    libstdc++ before this package's code ever ran (a well-documented
    reticulate failure mode, not fixable from within the affected process).
    Running each operation in a brand-new subprocess, with its own
    environment set at that subprocess's own exec() time, avoids this
    entirely and makes library(caEditR) plus a direct function call behave
    identically regardless of what else has been loaded into the calling
    R session.
